label Day3.sylvia
# story entry day=3
# art scene draft.reading.early
# story challenge {"challenge_id":"Day3.sylvia.challenge","playable_marker_id":"Day3.sylvia.playable","end_marker_id":"Day3.sylvia.end","board_profile_id":"week.canonical_solo","targets":{"never_started":"target.Day3.sylvia.result.A","unfinished":"target.Day3.sylvia.result.B","won":"target.Day3.sylvia.result.C","lost":"target.Day3.sylvia.result.D","perfect":"target.Day3.sylvia.result.E"}}
\“I'll come with you.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.001
\“And Lavinia?” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.002
\“I'll tell her now.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.003
\With one hand still warm inside Sylvia's coat pocket, the other hand sends the message\: I'm spending the evening with Sylvia. I won't be at the library at six. Sorry for the late change. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.004
\Sylvia keeps my hand inside her pocket all the way up the hill stairs. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.005
\In the quiet north room, the overhead lights are off; only the glass display case in the center is lit. Inside sits the cross-section model of a pale three-storey house—every window dark except for a tiny amber bulb in the attic and a two-inch painted figure wedged between an upstairs sill and the drainpipe. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.006
\“All those rooms,” I say, “and it stays by the pipe.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.007
\“Perhaps it likes the view.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.008
\“The rent must be reasonable.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.009
\Sylvia laughs; our reflections overlap on the glass panel. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.010
\Along the front of the case runs a row of folding wooden viewing stools. Sylvia pulls the end stool down. Before she has slipped her coat off one shoulder, the wooden seat snaps back up with a hollow knock against the back of her knees. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.011
\“Help,” she says. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.012
\One hand holds the wooden seat flat while the other takes the coat from her shoulder. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.013
\“Thank you.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.014
\She sits, drawing the coat across both our laps the way she did on the gallery bench, her shoulder resting warm against mine. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.015
\Behind the glass in front of us, the cross-section house glows softly\: miniature staircases, paper-thin doors, and in the ground-floor hall, a tiny open cupboard under the stairs where a second painted figure stands behind a half-shut door. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.016
\“A terrible place to hide,” I murmur. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.017
\“I'd live in the cupboard,” Sylvia whispers. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.018
\“No one would find you.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.019
\“You wouldn't have to look very hard.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.020
\Under the shared coat across our knees, her hand finds mine, slides her fingers across the palm, and settles there. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.021
\Inside the miniature house, the tiny bulb on the landing brightens by a step, throwing a thread of yellow light across the painted cupboard door. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.022
\Sylvia leans half an inch closer to the glass. Her lips part slightly; her eyes follow the miniature staircase as if someone were about to come down it, her hand resting forgotten in mine. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.023
\A stool beside her in the dark. Anyone could have sat here. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.024
\“Sylvia.” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.025
\“Mm?” #id:line.Day3.sylvia.026
\Step in front of the glass so she has to look at me instead. #id:line.Day3.sylvia.027
